From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gert Pellin <gert@pellin.be>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 20:06:30 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] Add surface database and surface classification of OSM
 ways

A surface database is a SQLite file with the highways of an OpenStreetMap
extract (.osm.pbf), split into chunks of up to 16 segments and indexed
with an R*Tree. It is built by CSurfaceDbBuilder, which reads the extract
with a small built-in PBF reader (zlib blobs, dense nodes, ways), so no
new library is needed.

CSurface classifies a way's highway/surface/tracktype/footway tags into a
surface class (paved, semi-paved, unpaved, unknown), a detailed surface
and a way type. CSurfaceDb looks up the way at a position as the majority
of the ways sampled every ~10 m in its ~20 m cell, with the nearest way
within 25 m as fallback. CSurfaceAnalyzer samples a track every 20 m,
smooths single-sample blips at crossings and sums the lengths.

The command line tool qms_surfacedb builds a database without the GUI.

Unit tests (BUILD_TESTING=ON, ctest) check the classification and the
cell lookup case by case against the reference implementation they were
ported from, the PBF reader and builder on a small fixture, the analysis
on synthetic data and, if the data is present, a real track against the
reference's numbers.
